
[38;5;231m⏺[39m That answer changes the classification. An identifier that doesn't exist yet, persists, and is consumed by forms
  across the app is a new subsystem with a stored data format — not a parameter on one function. Per the one-way
  ratchet, I'm [1mupgrading this from bounded to architectural[0m: questions, approaches, a sectioned design, then a written
  spec before any code. The [38;5;153mlogin()[39m signature change is the last and smallest part of that.

  [1mThe fork (first question):[0m what the ID is anchored to decides everything downstream — where it's stored, whether the
  backend can trust it, and whether it survives a logout or a new browser.

  - [1mDevice/browser ID[0m — minted client-side on first visit, stored locally, same value for anyone using that browser.
    Easy, zero backend work. It identifies a browser, not a person: shared machines collide, a new browser looks like a
    new user, and the value is user-editable so the server can't trust it for anything that matters.
  - [1mServer-assigned account ID[0m — the backend returns it on successful login; the app stores and replays it. Actually
    identifies the person and is trustworthy server-side. Requires the login API (currently a stub at [38;5;153mapp.js:2[39m) to
    return it, and it doesn't exist before first login.
  - [1mPer-session ID[0m — minted at login, dies when the session does. Good for correlating one visit's events; no
    cross-session continuity, so "who logged in" only holds within a visit.

  My recommendation: server-assigned, with the caveat that the API here is a stub — if there's no real backend to
  change, that path is blocked and device-ID becomes the honest fallback, clearly labeled as untrusted. Persisting an
  identifier also has a privacy dimension (it's tracking data in local storage); I'll raise that as its own question
  once this is settled.
